<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/AppTheme">

        <activity
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|locale|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|uiMode"
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:screenOrientation="sensorLandscape"
            android:label="@string/title_activity_main"
            android:theme="@style/AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:exported="true">

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths"></meta-data>
        </provider>
    </application>

    <!--
      PERMISSIONS — this list is COMPLETE and deliberately minimal.

      The GAME is entirely offline: it bundles every asset and fetches nothing.
      Both permissions below exist solely for the in-app updater, which pulls CI
      builds from GitHub Releases so new builds install from inside the app
      instead of from a dev server on a PC. See Updater.java.

      No storage, location, camera, microphone, contacts, SMS, call-log, phone,
      accessibility, notification-listener, overlay or query-all-packages
      permission is requested, and no library adds one during manifest merge —
      Capacitor's own manifest declares none and there are no Cordova plugins.
      There are also no <service> and no <receiver> declarations anywhere: the
      only component beyond MainActivity is the FileProvider below, and the
      updater's install-status receiver is registered at RUNTIME, on the
      application context, and only when an install is actually started — so it
      exists for the life of that process and dies with it. Nothing of this app
      runs in the background, and nothing survives the process, which is the
      property that matters here. (This used to claim it was unregistered when
      the install finished; there is no unregisterReceiver call and there never
      was. A manifest comment that describes code that does not exist is worse
      than no comment, because this file is the thing people audit.)

      Keep it that way. Every permission added here is a Play Protect heuristic
      taken on, and this app is sideloaded, so it has no store reputation to
      offset one.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!--
      THE ONE UNAVOIDABLE FLAG.

      This is what lets the UPDATE button work, and it is also the single
      strongest signal behind Play Protect's "Harmful app blocked" warning on a
      sideloaded build: an app that downloads an APK and installs it is
      structurally identical to a dropper, and a scanner cannot tell our intent
      from theirs. Everything else in this project is arranged to avoid ADDING to
      that suspicion — release-signed rather than debug-signed, no background
      components, no obfuscation, no second network use.

      Removing this permission is possible and would very likely clear the
      warning, but it costs the dev loop: the updater would have to hand the
      downloaded APK to the system installer via a browser download instead, and
      the owner would confirm the install in the notification shade rather than
      in the game. That is a product decision, not a cleanup — do not remove it
      as part of a tidy-up.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES" />
</manifest>
